```html
<div id="progress"></div>
<article>…</article>
```

```css
#progress {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0 0 auto 0;          /* top, full width */
  height: 4px;
  background: rebeccapurple;
  transform-origin: 0 50%;
  transform: scaleX(0);       /* base state for non-supporting browsers */
  pointer-events: none;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    @keyframes grow-progress {
      from { transform: scaleX(0); }
      to   { transform: scaleX(1); }
    }
    #progress {
      animation: grow-progress auto linear;
      animation-timeline: scroll(root);  /* must come after the shorthand */
    }
  }
}
```

- **Animate `transform`, not `width`.** `scaleX` runs on the compositor.
- **Put `animation-timeline` after the `animation` shorthand.** The shorthand resets it otherwise.
- **Leave the bar out of the accessibility tree.** An empty decorative `div` needs nothing extra. Add `aria-hidden="true"` if you put content inside it.
- **Reduced motion:** the guide wraps the animation in `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`, so those users see no bar. A progress bar is arguably not motion-heavy, so you could drop that wrapper. I kept the guide's default.

## Fallback (optional)

The guide says a purely decorative indicator can simply do nothing in unsupported browsers. If you want a fallback, use this:

```js
if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
  const bar = document.querySelector('#progress');
  const update = () => {
    const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight;
    bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? scrollY / max : 0})`;
  };
  addEventListener('scroll', update, { passive: true });
  update();
}
```

I changed two things from the guide's snippet. The bar's base styles sit outside `@supports`, so the fallback has something to draw. The script also guards against divide-by-zero on short pages. Don't use the `scroll-timeline-polyfill` package. The guide says it's incomplete and has known issues.

## Tracking the article instead of the page

`scroll(root)` measures the whole document, including header, footer and comments. To track only the article, use a view timeline on it:

```css
article { view-timeline: --read block; }
#progress {
  animation: grow-progress auto linear;
  animation-timeline: --read;
  animation-range: contain 0% contain 100%;
}
```

This needs `timeline-scope: --read` on a shared ancestor, because `#progress` isn't a descendant of `article`. I didn't find this variant in the bundled guide, so check it against current docs before relying on it.

### Modern Web Guidance
- **`scroll-progress-indicator`**: I used `@supports (animation-timeline: scroll())` with a `transform: scaleX` animation, and put `animation-timeline` after the shorthand so the shorthand can't reset it ("**DO** use the correct declaration order: When using the `animation` shorthand property, declare `animation-timeline` *after* it").

**Use CSS scroll-driven animations.** They've been Baseline "newly available" since 2026-10-27. Chrome and Edge have supported them since 115, Safari since 26, and Firefox since 159. This comes from the bundled Modern Web Guidance guide `ui-atoms/scroll-progress-indicator.md`. I didn't run the CLI, so this is the bundled 1.0.0 copy.

**HTML**
```html
<div id="progress"></div>
```

**CSS**
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    @keyframes grow-progress {
      from { transform: scaleX(0); }
      to   { transform: scaleX(1); }
    }

    #progress {
      position: fixed;
      inset: 0 0 auto 0;      /* top/left/right = 0 */
      height: 4px;
      background: red;
      transform-origin: 0 50%;

      animation: grow-progress auto linear;
      animation-timeline: scroll(root block); /* after the shorthand */
    }
  }
}
```

**Key points**
- **Animate `transform: scaleX()`, not `width`.** It runs on the compositor, so it stays smooth.
- **Declare `animation-timeline` after the `animation` shorthand.** The shorthand resets it if it comes first.
- **`scroll(root block)` tracks the page scroll.** The default `scroll()` uses the nearest ancestor scroller, so be explicit if the bar sits inside another scroller.
- **Wrap it in `@supports`.** Browsers without support never show the bar.
- **Wrap it in `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`.** Users who want less motion don't get the bar.
- **Leave the bar empty.** An empty styled div needs no ARIA. If you add text or an image to it, add `aria-hidden="true"`.
- **Don't use `scroll-timeline-polyfill`.** The guide calls it incomplete and buggy.

**Fallback (optional)**
A reading bar is decorative, so you can skip the fallback. If you want one for older browsers, run a scroll listener only when native support is missing:

```html
<script>
  if (!CSS.supports('animation-timeline', 'scroll()')) {
    const bar = document.querySelector('#progress');
    addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - innerHeight;
      bar.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? scrollY / max : 0})`;
    }, { passive: true });
  }
</script>
```

The fallback also needs the base styles outside `@supports`, with `transform: scaleX(0)`. The fallback script is the guide's version with two small changes: it guards against dividing by zero, and it adds `{ passive: true }`.

**Progress through the article only**
The snippet above tracks the whole page. To track just the article, use a view timeline on the `<article>`:
```css
article { view-timeline: --article block; timeline-scope: --article; }
#progress { animation-timeline: --article; animation-range: contain 0% contain 100%; }
```
